<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Magenta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-italian-war-of-independence">Second Italian War of Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Magenta was fought on 4 June 1859 near Magenta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-italian-war-of-independence">Second Italian War of Independence</a>. A French army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-de-macmahon">Patrice de MacMahon</a>, with Sardinian support, defeated the Austrians under Ferenc Gyulai, who abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. MacMahon was made Duke of Magenta, and the new magenta dye was named after the battle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-magenta.jpg" alt="Battle of Magenta" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adolphe Yvon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adolphe_Yvon_-_Batalla_de_Magenta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>From 1 to 3 June 1859 the French and Piedmontese pursued the Austrian 2nd Army to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino-river">Ticino River</a>, the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. The Austrians under Marshal Ferenc Gyulay set up a defensive position at Magenta, using the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naviglio-grande">Naviglio Grande</a>, a canal that could be crossed only at four bridges. Gyulay had 68,000 men in the I, II, III and VII Korps. The French had about 50,000, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manfredo-fanti">Manfredo Fanti</a> added another 12,000. After the Battle of Turbigo, Camou crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a>, followed by MacMahon, and with Espinasse they crossed the canal on bridges at Bernate Ticino and Boffalora sopra Ticino, which placed them north of Magenta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The country was confined, a spread of orchards cut by streams and irrigation canals, and it prevented elaborate manoeuvre. The Austrians turned every house into a miniature fortress. At noon on 4 June 1859 MacMahon met elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a>'s II Korps, and the Imperial Guard made contact with the Austrians from Buffalora to Magenta. At 2 pm the Guard Zouaves crossed the canal in boats and established a bridgehead. Gyulay, told of the French attack by Eduard Clam-Gallas, sent Schwarzenberg's III Korps from Robecco sul Naviglio against the French right flank, but Canrobert arrived in time to reinforce the Guard.</p><p>From 3.30 to 5.30 pm MacMahon attacked the Austrian I and II Korps. By 6.30 pm the Austrians began a fighting withdrawal while the French advanced into Magenta and beyond. By 10 pm the Austrian 2nd Army was retreating towards Abbiategrasso. The brunt of the fighting fell on 5,000 grenadiers of the French Imperial Guard, still mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">First Empire</a> uniforms. It was not a particularly large battle but was a decisive victory for the Franco-Sardinian alliance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 8 June and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> a few days later. Gyulay withdrew to the Chiese River east of Milan and resigned on 16 June. Patrice de MacMahon was created Duke of Magenta for his part in the battle and later served as President of the French Third Republic. A dye discovered in 1859 was named magenta after the battle, apocryphally because its colour matched the blood-stained red trousers of French soldiers, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> boulevard, the Boulevard de Magenta, bears the name as well.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Magenta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>France and Piedmont defeated it at <b>Magenta</b> and Solferino in 1859, and in 1866 the Prussian army, using breech-loading rifles and railways, crushed it at the Battle of Königgrätz on 3 July.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In alliance with Napoleon III of France, Sardinia defeated Austria in 1859 at <b>Magenta</b> and Solferino and gained Lombardy; in 1860 plebiscites joined Tuscany, Parma, Modena and the Romagna, while Savoy and Nice were ceded to France as the price of French support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A contingent sent to the Crimean War in 1855 restored its standing, and with French help it won at <b>Magenta</b> and Solferino in 1859.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
